Let's simplify and make this more robust by having the Go application directly provide the necessary API base URL to the Next.js frontend running within the altgui webview.

Here's a refined build strategy and implementation plan:

Core Architectural Principles:

main.go as the Source of Truth for API Endpoint:

Your main Go application (main.go) determines the publicly accessible base URL for its core API (e.g., http://<public_ip_of_reverse_proxy>:<reverse_proxy_port>/api). This is the URL the Next.js frontend will ultimately use.
In-Memory Propagation to altgui.go:

When main.go initializes the altgui service (which is part of the main Go package), it passes this dynamically determined API base URL directly as a parameter.
altgui.go Injects Configuration into WebView:

The altgui.go service, which manages the webview/webview_go instance:
Serves the static Next.js application files on a local port (e.g., cfg.AltGUIPort).
Uses webview.Bind() to expose a Go function to the JavaScript context of the webview. This function will return the API base URL it received from main.go.
Centralized Reverse Proxy:

Your main application's reverse proxy remains the public-facing entry point. It routes:
Requests for the Next.js application (e.g., /, /static/...) to the local HTTP server managed by altgui.go.
Requests for the main backend API (e.g., /api/...) to the blockchain_server.go service.
Next.js Frontend (Runtime Fetch via WebView Binding):

The Next.js application, when loaded in the webview, calls the JavaScript function bound by altgui.go (e.g., window.getAppConfigFromGo()) to retrieve the API base URL.
Clean Next.js Build:

altgui/next.config.js has no build-time knowledge of the backend API's URL.
