Summary of this Approach:

main.go calculates the actual public-facing API base URL for the main backend services (e.g., http://public-proxy-addr/api).
This URL is passed to altgui.go when it's initialized.
altgui.go stores this URL and uses webview.Bind("getAppConfigFromGo", ...) to make it available to the JavaScript running inside the webview.
The Next.js app calls window.getAppConfigFromGo() to get this URL at runtime.
All API calls from Next.js use this dynamically obtained URL, which correctly targets your main backend API via the reverse proxy.
The /app-config HTTP endpoint in altgui.go is no longer necessary for the primary flow.
This method is direct, avoids circular dependencies, and is well-suited for an application where the frontend is embedded within a Go-managed webview. It correctly separates the concerns of serving the static UI (altgui.go's local HTTP server) and providing the runtime configuration for the actual backend API